0050 — a baked track holds up to 65,535 segments, where the ANIM TABLE's
one-byte segment count stopped it at 255, and the core finds the segment a
tick falls in by binary search instead of walking from the first.

Where the limit was, measured on the pin with 0001-0041 applied: `segCount`
is the second byte of a track header (`contracts/spec/spec.ts`, ANIM TABLE).
`encodeStyleTable` throws "animation track needs 1..255 segments" above it,
and `StyleTable::parse` (`engine/core/src/style.rs`) reads the byte and
refuses 0. The baker (`framework/compiler/animation.ts`) has no limit of its
own: a keyframe set of 1,000 stops over 20 s bakes 1,000 segments, and the
build then fails at the encoder. The next ceiling is the timeline itself —
period, delay, t0 and t1 are u16 frame counts, 65,535 ticks or 18 min 12 s
at 60 Hz — and it bounds the segment count as well, because every segment
spans at least one frame. So the count is widened to exactly that and no
further.

The format. A `segCount` byte of 0 (`ANIM_SEG_COUNT_WIDE`), which every core
before this refuses, is followed by a u16 count. The encoder writes the wide
form only above 255, and both decoders refuse a wide count of 255 or less,
so a table has one encoding and a table with no track over 255 segments is
byte for byte what it was: every bundle and pak of the motion repository's
38-composition digest ledger built identical before and after this patch.
A core without it refuses a table carrying a wide track as a whole rather
than reading the bytes as something else.

The lookup. `sample_track` walked from the first segment on every tick, so a
track's per-tick cost grew with its length and a film's total with the
square of its length. It now takes `partition_point` on `t1`: the first
segment ending after the tick is the only one that can hold it. That is the
walk's answer only for sorted, disjoint segments, which spec.ts has always
required and the baker has always produced, so `StyleTable::parse` now
refuses a track whose segments overlap, run backwards or have zero length
instead of trusting the order. No table the baker writes is refused: every
core test and every composition in the motion repository loads. Measured on
a 7.5-minute 320x180 composition with one camera track (six tracks of
13,500 segments) and nothing else long: a full trace took 4.0 s of user CPU
walking and 2.4 s searching, every frame hash equal.

Tests, each seen red under the edit named:

- `engine/core/src/tests.rs` `a_track_over_255_segments_plays_every_segment`:
  255, 256 and 30,000 two-tick segments, the value checked at every tick.
  Red with the parser refusing the wide form.
- `a_wide_segment_count_that_fits_one_byte_is_refused`: red with the
  one-encoding check removed.
- `sample_track_finds_what_a_walk_from_the_start_finds`: 200 seeded tracks
  of 1..600 segments with gaps, one-tick, eased and bezier segments, colour
  and scalar payloads, every tick against the walk kept verbatim in the
  test. Red with the search's predicate `t1 < lt` in place of `t1 <= lt`.
- `a_track_whose_segments_overlap_or_run_backwards_is_refused`: red with
  the order check removed.
- `tests/anim-long-tracks.test.ts`: the 2-byte header at 255 and the 4-byte
  one at 256, the decoder reading back 1,000 and 32,767 segments, the
  refusals at 65,536 and at a wide 255, and a 1,000-stop theme keyframe set
  through the baker, the encoder and the decoder. Three of the four red
  with the encoder writing the one-byte form for every count.

